<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · Protest and freedom · 1950 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Senzeni Na?</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A slow, sad song asking &quot;What have we done?&quot;, sung by South Africans at funerals and protests against apartheid.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Black South Africans of the anti-apartheid struggle</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xhosa</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>South Africa</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>at least since the 1950s</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional, origins unclear (sung at least since the 1950s))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senzeni-na">Senzeni Na?</a>&quot; (&quot;What Have We Done?&quot;), also spelled Senzenina, is a South African folk song of the struggle against apartheid, sung in Xhosa. Its origins are unclear; it has been sung at least since the 1950s. It was one of several songs of a mournful kind that became popular among anti-apartheid activists in the 1960s, and it reached the height of its popularity in the 1980s.</p><p>The song was sung at funerals, at demonstrations and in churches. Its question is repeated again and again, which the musician Sibongile Khumalo described as leaving the listener with a sense of desolation. No single agreed text exists, and some versions are angrier than others. The activist Duma Ndlovu compared its influence to that of the American protest song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/we-shall-overcome">We Shall Overcome</a>&quot;, and Pete Seeger already had an adaptation of it in his repertoire in the 1950s and 1960s.</p><p>A recording of the song as sung at the funeral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko">Steve Biko</a> can be heard at the end of the album version of Peter Gabriel's &quot;Biko&quot;. It was sung in the funeral scene of the anti-apartheid film The Power of One and over the opening credits of the film In My Country, and Kenneth Nkosi sang it in a medley in the 2012 film Mad Buddies. Its music carries an adaptation of Isaac Watts's hymn &quot;When I Survey the Wondrous Cross&quot; in the Mennonite Hymnal: A Worship Book. The Zimbabwean poet Albert Nyathi wrote a different song of the same title on the day the activist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chris-hani">Chris Hani</a> died.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A lament in the form of a question, asked over and over by people suffering under apartheid: what wrong have they done to be treated so. The repetition gives it the weight of mourning at a graveside, and in some versions the grief turns to anger and defiance.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Xhosa</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Xhosa</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Senzeni na?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What have we done?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sono sethu, ubumnyama?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our sin, is it blackness?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sono sethu yinyaniso?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our sin, is it the truth?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Sibulawayo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We are being killed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="xh">Mayibuye i Africa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let Africa come back</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional isiXhosa song (one version among several); as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senzeni_Na%3F" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'Senzeni na?' is literally 'What have we done?'.</li><li>line 1.2: 'ubumnyama' is darkness or blackness; in this song it is read as being Black. The question mark makes it 'is our sin that we are black?'</li><li>line 1.4: 'Sibulawayo' is a passive, 'we are being killed'.</li><li>line 1.5: 'Mayibuye i Africa' (Mayibuye iAfrika) is the liberation movement's call 'Let Africa return', i.e. let Africa be given back to its people.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="SJP79Zon3Ek" data-title="Senzeni Na?: Cape Town Youth Choir, Monde Mdingi solo"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Cape Town Youth Choir, Monde Mdingi solo</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Cape Town Camerata. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJP79Zon3Ek" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Biko (Peter Gabriel, album version)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording from Steve Biko's funeral heard at its end</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Power of One (film)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in the funeral scene</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In My Country (film)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung over the opening credits</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pete Seeger's adaptation</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1950s-1960s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adaptation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">When I Survey the Wondrous Cross, in the Mennonite Hymnal: A Worship Book</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same music</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mad Buddies (film; Kenneth Nkosi, in a medley with &quot;Transkaroo&quot;)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2012</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mars trilogy (Kim Stanley Robinson)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a city and Part 7 of a book named after it</span></li></ul></section>
